{# Shared between the Docker pihole role (renders on daniel-server, where the default is
   right) and roles/k8s/pihole's ConfigMap (renders on daniel-box, which must pin the
   wildcard to daniel-server per slice-6 D7 — the LAN wildcard does NOT move with B3). #}
{# BT3 (2026-08-10): the wildcard answers the cluster ingress VIP on every Pi-hole — the
   cluster edge serves k8s natives and reverse-bridges Docker-hosted names. The old
   default(server_ip) fallback is gone on purpose: it made the answer depend on which host
   rendered the file. #}
{# DECIDED (2026-08-24): a matching PUBLIC wildcard `*.local.<domain>` -> this same VIP is
   authoritative on Cloudflare, and it STAYS. It is not managed by this repo — cloudflare-ddns
   handles only the apex, terraria and valheim — so it reads as a stray record leaking the
   internal ingress VIP to anyone who asks 1.1.1.1, including for names that do not exist.
   Removing it was proposed and is WRONG, because two paths depend on it:

     1. CoreDNS forwards `policy sequential` with health_check/max_fails 2 (see
        roles/setup/k3s/templates/coredns-corefile.j2), Pi-hole first and the public resolvers
        after. During a Pi-hole outage — the exact case that failover exists for — in-cluster
        `.local.` lookups reach 1.1.1.1 and still land on the right VIP BECAUSE of the public
        record. Drop it and that degradation becomes NXDOMAIN.
     2. daniel-box's own host resolver falls through to the public resolvers whenever Pi-hole
        does not answer (roles/setup/k3s/templates/resolv.conf.j2 — Pi-hole first, 1.1.1.1
        behind it, re-tried in that order on every query). Until 2026-09-01 that resolver was
        pinned to the public servers UNCONDITIONALLY, so this was a permanent dependency;
        now it is the same failover dependency as (1), on the same outage. Drop the record
        and host-level `.local.` lookups on the control-plane node go NXDOMAIN for exactly
        as long as Pi-hole is down.

   The exposure is reconnaissance only: 10.0.0.240 is unroutable from the internet, so the
   record discloses an RFC1918 address and the existence of the naming scheme, nothing more.
   ACME is unaffected either way — issuance uses the DNS-01 TXT challenge, not this A record. #}
address=/local.{{ domain }}/{{ pihole_wildcard_ip | default(k3s_metallb_ingress_vip) }}
address=/local.{{ domain }}/::

# k3s-hosted services answer with the cluster's MetalLB ingress VIP instead of the wildcard
# above. dnsmasq resolves the most specific `address=` match, so these override
# local.<domain> for exactly these names and nothing else.
#
# Derived from daniel-box's inventory rather than hand-listed: slice 2 moves ~33 more
# services, and a hand-maintained list here is a second source of truth that silently rots
# the first time someone forgets it. Entries without a `hostname` (traefik itself) have no
# route of their own and are skipped.
#
# `extra_hostnames` covers an entry that fronts MORE THAN ONE routed workload — claude-otel
# runs Grafana, Prometheus, Loki and Tempo under a single containers_list entry, so its
# `hostname` can only name one of them. Without this, a second workload's IngressRoute exists
# in the cluster but its name falls through to the local.<domain> wildcard above, resolves to
# daniel-server, and the Docker Traefik answers its own 404 — which reads like a broken route
# rather than a missing DNS record.
{% for svc in hostvars[k8s_primary_node].containers_list | default([]) | filter_by_platform('k8s') %}
{% if svc.hostname is defined %}
address=/{{ svc.hostname }}.local.{{ domain }}/{{ k3s_metallb_ingress_vip }}
{% endif %}
{% for extra in svc.extra_hostnames | default([]) %}
address=/{{ extra }}.local.{{ domain }}/{{ k3s_metallb_ingress_vip }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

# daniel-pi is LAN-only. Use a private `.lan` TLD (resolves only here, via Pi-hole) instead
# of a <domain> subdomain: Traefik sets HSTS with stsIncludeSubdomains on the real domain,
# which force-upgrades any *.<domain> name to HTTPS and breaks the Pi's plain-HTTP services
# (SSL_ERROR_RX_RECORD_TOO_LONG). `.lan` sidesteps HSTS entirely. Reach the Pi's LAN-bound
# services at daniel-pi.lan:<port>. IP via hostvars since the Pi isn't the host running Pi-hole.
host-record=daniel-pi.lan,{{ hostvars['daniel-pi'].server_ip }}
